#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1140.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167925} :activity/id #uuid "7adce642-b854-586d-bc06-8e89b32c3f0d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049850 :topic/id #uuid "18b6bb33-dbf4-537c-bb41-f5a6dca39be8" :topic/math-academy-id 3224} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167925 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168040 :tutorial/content "When an integrand contains a product of trigonometric functions, we can often rewrite that product as a sum using the sum and difference formulas. Then, the integral becomes a sum of simpler integrals.\n\nFirst, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get \n\n$$\n\\cos(\\alpha-\\beta) + \\cos(\\alpha+\\beta) = 2 \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta.\n$$\n\nNow, solving for $\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta,$ we obtain the following **product-to-sum identity**:\n\n$$\n\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\cos(\\alpha-\\beta)+\\cos(\\alpha+\\beta)\\right)\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\cos(5x)\\cos(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the identity above,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x-3x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x+3x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :tutorial/id #uuid "c26de959-d4f8-59d7-987a-b054cf26fe18" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14665 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9048252a-25e5-52f9-bd7e-090c6a659879" :step/math-academy-id 594773 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167926}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167926 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166952 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166569 :question/id #uuid "177a0110-424b-5ac3-a24e-e817cbcf479c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21823"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3cb2fcb-3457-5a89-8968-6c0f2c2cfa83" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048319} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050783}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314947} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546996} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547060} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547064}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Cosines"} :step/id #uuid "58ba6660-e16f-5452-9a6a-841f7b3660e9" :step/math-academy-id 594774 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167927}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167927 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168041 :tutorial/content "Similar to the above, when an integrand contains a product of sines, we can rewrite that product as a sum of cosines using the sum and difference formulas for cosine. Then, the integral becomes a sum of simpler integrals.\n\nFirst, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\cos(\\alpha-\\beta) - \\cos(\\alpha+\\beta) = 2 \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta.\n$$\n\nNow, solving for $\\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta,$ we obtain the following *product-to-sum identity*:\n\n$$\n\\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\cos(\\alpha-\\beta)-\\cos(\\alpha+\\beta)\\right)\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\sin(7x)\\sin(4x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the identity above,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x-4x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x+4x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{22}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :tutorial/id #uuid "0b307dbf-5537-546f-8a0c-3ad325959aca" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14666 :tutorial/title "Integrating a Product of Sines"} :step/id #uuid "038580ae-a8f9-5da4-a4d6-a1de5dc8cfea" :step/math-academy-id 594777 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167928}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167928 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166953 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166570 :question/id #uuid "ebf57ac7-29e3-595e-8a72-032e10c470aa" :question/math-academy-id "e-21824"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a371de4-7c3f-5687-bd3a-d3ab5ab803ce" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167278} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314952} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314955} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377333} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546975}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Sines"} :step/id #uuid "22c6f08d-2044-5989-8b6c-33807f441aef" :step/math-academy-id 594778 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167929}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167929 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168042 :tutorial/content "Next, when an integrand contains a product of sine and cosine, we can rewrite that product as a sum of sines using the sum and difference formulas for sine. Then, the integral becomes a sum of simpler integrals.\n\nFirst, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get\n\n$$\n\\sin(\\alpha+\\beta) + \\sin(\\alpha-\\beta) = 2 \\sin \\alpha \\cos \\beta.\n$$\n\nNow, solving for $\\sin \\alpha \\cos \\beta,$ we obtain the following *product-to-sum identity*:\n\n$$\n\\sin \\alpha \\cos \\beta = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\sin(\\alpha+\\beta)+\\sin(\\alpha-\\beta)\\right)\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\sin(8x)\\cos(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the identity above,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x+2x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x-2x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{20}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10x)-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :tutorial/id #uuid "1e3af3c5-49cd-524e-8614-b45a35212820" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14667 :tutorial/title "Integrating a Product of Sine and Cosine"} :step/id #uuid "6a2daae3-51d3-5fc0-afb6-806b9e8b507e" :step/math-academy-id 594781 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167930}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167930 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166954 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166571 :question/id #uuid "14466847-40e1-5a4f-b50a-23b62590f0a2" :question/math-academy-id "e-21825"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d3b7f35-fcd1-5443-b0ea-0b06e016e0c3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049773}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314960} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314962} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377339} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547027}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Sine and Cosine"} :step/id #uuid "e2c70847-dbf9-50ac-a77c-3c64374510b4" :step/math-academy-id 594782}] :activity/title "Integrating Products of Trigonometric Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186167004}]}
